  <h1>Growing Corn in the Philippines: Planting, Fertilizing, Weeding, and Pest Control</h1>
  <p>A field journal from Glenn Pansensoy — following the corn from seed to tassel through rain, fog, and the quiet work of the season.</p>
</header>

<main>

  <p class="video-credit">Video by Glenn Pansensoy</p>

  <h2>Planting After the Dry Spell</h2>
  <p>The dry spell finally ended. A heavy downpour the night before broke the week-long stretch of dry weather that had left the soil hard and the plants waiting. The rain soaked the parched rows, filled the furrows, and left the morning air cool and sweet. With the land refreshed, I brought eight hardworking members of our team into the field to plant the corn. We moved quickly but carefully opening furrows, placing the seeds at the proper depth, covering them gently with soil, and firming each row so moisture would stay close to the seed. The team worked in rhythm, trading tips and smiling even with damp earth clinging to their boots. I checked spacing and seed placement, made small adjustments for the slightly softer soil, and marked the rows for future irrigation and fertilization.</p>

