        <section id="subprocess" aria-labelledby="subprocess-heading">
            <div class="page-col">
                <div class="section-intro">
                    <h2 id="subprocess-heading">💻 Running External Commands — <code>subprocess</code></h2>
                    <p>Automation often means orchestrating other programs. Learn to run, control, and capture output from external commands.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="oop-block">
                    <h3>💻 4.1 <code>subprocess.run()</code> — The Modern Way</h3>
                    <p><code>subprocess.run()</code> is the recommended way to run external commands in Python 3.5+.</p>

                    <h4 style="color:var(--blue-lt);font-size:0.8rem;margin-top:0.8rem;margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Step-by-step explanation:</h4>
                    <ul class="step-list">
                        <li><strong>Step 1:</strong> Import <code>subprocess</code>.</li>
                        <li><strong>Step 2:</strong> Use <code>subprocess.run()</code> with a list of arguments.</li>
                        <li><strong>Step 3:</strong> Capture output with <code>capture_output=True</code>.</li>
                        <li><strong>Step 4:</strong> Access <code>result.stdout</code> and <code>result.stderr</code> as bytes or strings with <code>text=True</code>.</li>
                        <li><strong>Step 5:</strong> Check the return code with <code>result.returncode</code>.</li>
                        <li><strong>Step 6:</strong> Use <code>check=True</code> to raise an exception on non‑zero exit codes.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <div class="code-container" data-code=''import subprocess

def subprocess_demo():
    # Run a simple command
    result = subprocess.run(["echo", "Hello from subprocess!"], capture_output=True, text=True)
    print(f"✅ Command output: {result.stdout.strip()}")
    print(f"Return code: {result.returncode}")

    # Run a command that fails
    try:
        subprocess.run(["false"], check=True)
    except subprocess.CalledProcessError as e:
        print(f"❌ Command failed with exit code: {e.returncode}")

    # Capture stderr
    result = subprocess.run(["ls", "/nonexistent"], capture_output=True, text=True)
    print(f"stderr: {result.stderr.strip()}")

subprocess_demo()''>
