        <section id="scheduling" aria-labelledby="scheduling-heading">
            <div class="page-col">
                <div class="section-intro">
                    <h2 id="scheduling-heading">⏰ Scheduling Tasks — Run on a Schedule</h2>
                    <p>Automation runs on its own schedule. Learn to schedule tasks with Python''s <code>schedule</code> library and <code>time</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="oop-block">
                    <h3>⏰ 5.1 Basic Scheduling with <code>schedule</code></h3>
                    <p>The <code>schedule</code> library provides a human‑friendly API for scheduling tasks.</p>

                    <h4 style="color:var(--blue-lt);font-size:0.8rem;margin-top:0.8rem;margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Step-by-step explanation:</h4>
                    <ul class="step-list">
                        <li><strong>Step 1:</strong> Import <code>schedule</code> and <code>time</code>.</li>
                        <li><strong>Step 2:</strong> Define a function to run on schedule.</li>
                        <li><strong>Step 3:</strong> Use <code>schedule.every().</code> syntax to set the schedule.</li>
                        <li><strong>Step 4:</strong> In an infinite loop, call <code>schedule.run_pending()</code>.</li>
                        <li><strong>Step 5:</strong> Use <code>time.sleep(1)</code> to avoid busy‑waiting.</li>
                        <li><strong>Step 6:</strong> Run the scheduler for a limited number of iterations for demo purposes.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <div class="code-container" data-code=''import schedule
import time

def scheduled_task():
    print(f"⏰ Task ran at {time.strftime(&#39;%H:%M:%S&#39;)}")

def scheduler_demo():
    print("⏳ Starting scheduler (runs for 5 seconds)")

    # Schedule every 2 seconds
    schedule.every(2).seconds.do(scheduled_task)

    start = time.time()
    while time.time() - start < 5:
        schedule.run_pending()
        time.sleep(0.5)

    print("✅ Scheduler stopped")

scheduler_demo()''>
