                <div class="oop-block">
                    <h3>🌐 12.1 Website Automation with <code>Selenium</code></h3>
                    <p>Selenium drives a real browser exactly like a human would: it opens pages, fills in forms, clicks buttons, and reads back whatever appears on screen. This is the foundation of login bots, form-fillers, and scrapers for pages that need JavaScript to render.</p>

                    <h4 style="color:var(--blue-lt);font-size:0.8rem;margin-top:0.8rem;margin-bottom:0.4rem;">What this program does:</h4>
                    <p>Logs into a sample website, types a username and password, submits the form, waits for the page to load, and reads the welcome message that comes back.</p>

                    <div class="code-container no-run">
                        <div class="code-header">
                            <span style="color:var(--muted);">python</span>
                            <div class="code-actions">
                                <button class="code-btn copy-btn"><i class="fas fa-copy"></i> Copy</button>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-snippet"><pre><span class="kw">from</span> selenium <span class="kw">import</span> webdriver
<span class="kw">from</span> selenium.webdriver.common.by <span class="kw">import</span> By
<span class="kw">from</span> selenium.webdriver.common.keys <span class="kw">import</span> Keys
<span class="kw">import</span> time

<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">website_automation_demo</span>():
    driver = webdriver.Chrome()

    <span class="kw">try</span>:
        driver.get(<span class="st">"https://example.com/login"</span>)

        username_field = driver.find_element(By.NAME, <span class="st">"username"</span>)
        username_field.send_keys(<span class="st">"automation_user"</span>)

        password_field = driver.find_element(By.NAME, <span class="st">"password"</span>)
        password_field.send_keys(<span class="st">"secure_password"</span>)

        password_field.send_keys(Keys.RETURN)
        time.sleep(2)

        welcome = driver.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, <span class="st">"h1.welcome"</span>).text
        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"✅ Logged in. Welcome message: {welcome}"</span>)

    <span class="kw">finally</span>:
        driver.quit()
        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"🧹 Browser closed"</span>)

website_automation_demo()</pre></div>
                    </div>

                    <h4 style="color:var(--blue-lt);font-size:0.8rem;margin-top:0.8rem;margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Line-by-line explanation:</h4>
                    <ul class="line-list">
                        <li><strong>Lines 1–3:</strong> Import <code>webdriver</code> (controls the browser), <code>By</code> (a set of strategies for locating elements, such as by name or CSS selector), and <code>Keys</code> (represents keyboard keys like Enter).</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 4:</strong> Import <code>time</code> so the script can pause while pages load.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 7:</strong> <code>webdriver.Chrome()</code> launches a real, visible Chrome window that Selenium controls — this is the browser "session".</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 9:</strong> <code>try:</code> begins a block that guarantees cleanup even if something goes wrong further down.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 10:</strong> <code>driver.get(url)</code> navigates the open browser to the login page, just like typing the URL yourself.</li>
                        <li><strong>Lines 12–13:</strong> <code>find_element(By.NAME, "username")</code> locates the input box whose HTML <code>name</code> attribute is <code>"username"</code>; <code>send_keys()</code> types text into it, character by character.</li>
                        <li><strong>Lines 15–16:</strong> Same idea for the password field — locate it by name, then type the password.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 18:</strong> <code>send_keys(Keys.RETURN)</code> simulates pressing the Enter key inside the password field, which submits the form.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 19:</strong> <code>time.sleep(2)</code> pauses for two seconds so the next page has time to load before we try to read it.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 21:</strong> Locates an element by CSS selector (<code>h1.welcome</code> means "an <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> tag with class <code>welcome</code>") and reads its visible text with <code>.text</code>.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 22:</strong> Prints the welcome message that was scraped from the page after login.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 24:</strong> <code>finally:</code> runs no matter what — even if the login failed or an element was not found.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 25:</strong> <code>driver.quit()</code> closes the browser window and ends the session, freeing memory.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <div class="note-warn">⚠️ Selenium needs a real Chrome/Firefox installation plus a matching driver (<code>pip install selenium</code>), so it cannot run inside the in-browser Pyodide runner on this page — copy it into your own terminal to try it. It will not execute if you press Run above.</div>
                </div>

                <div class="oop-block">
                    <h3>📱 12.2 Phone Automation with <code>ADB</code> (Android Debug Bridge)</h3>
                    <p>ADB is a command-line tool that ships with the Android SDK. Once a phone has USB debugging enabled and is plugged in (or connected over Wi-Fi), Python can drive it through <code>subprocess</code> — taking screenshots, tapping the screen, swiping, and launching apps.</p>

                    <h4 style="color:var(--blue-lt);font-size:0.8rem;margin-top:0.8rem;margin-bottom:0.4rem;">What this program does:</h4>
                    <p>Lists connected devices, pulls a screenshot from the phone to the computer, simulates a tap, simulates a swipe, and launches an app by its package name.</p>

                    <div class="code-container no-run">
                        <div class="code-header">
                            <span style="color:var(--muted);">python</span>
                            <div class="code-actions">
                                <button class="code-btn copy-btn"><i class="fas fa-copy"></i> Copy</button>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-snippet"><pre><span class="kw">import</span> subprocess

<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">phone_automation_demo</span>():
    devices = subprocess.run([<span class="st">"adb"</span>, <span class="st">"devices"</span>], capture_output=True, text=True)
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"📱 Connected devices:"</span>)
    <span class="fn">print</span>(devices.stdout)

    subprocess.run([<span class="st">"adb"</span>, <span class="st">"shell"</span>, <span class="st">"screencap"</span>, <span class="st">"-p"</span>, <span class="st">"/sdcard/screen.png"</span>])
    subprocess.run([<span class="st">"adb"</span>, <span class="st">"pull"</span>, <span class="st">"/sdcard/screen.png"</span>, <span class="st">"screen.png"</span>])
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"✅ Screenshot saved as screen.png"</span>)

    subprocess.run([<span class="st">"adb"</span>, <span class="st">"shell"</span>, <span class="st">"input"</span>, <span class="st">"tap"</span>, <span class="st">"500"</span>, <span class="st">"1000"</span>])
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"✅ Tapped at (500, 1000)"</span>)

    subprocess.run([<span class="st">"adb"</span>, <span class="st">"shell"</span>, <span class="st">"input"</span>, <span class="st">"swipe"</span>, <span class="st">"300"</span>, <span class="st">"1500"</span>, <span class="st">"300"</span>, <span class="st">"500"</span>, <span class="st">"300"</span>])
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"✅ Swiped up"</span>)

    subprocess.run([<span class="st">"adb"</span>, <span class="st">"shell"</span>, <span class="st">"monkey"</span>, <span class="st">"-p"</span>, <span class="st">"com.android.settings"</span>,
                     <span class="st">"-c"</span>, <span class="st">"android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"</span>, <span class="st">"1"</span>])
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"✅ Launched Settings app"</span>)

phone_automation_demo()</pre></div>
                    </div>

                    <h4 style="color:var(--blue-lt);font-size:0.8rem;margin-top:0.8rem;margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Line-by-line explanation:</h4>
                    <ul class="line-list">
                        <li><strong>Line 1:</strong> Import <code>subprocess</code> — the same module used earlier for running external commands. ADB itself is not a Python library; it''s a program we call from Python.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 4:</strong> Runs <code>adb devices</code>, which asks ADB to list every phone or emulator currently connected and authorized.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 8:</strong> Runs <code>adb shell screencap -p /sdcard/screen.png</code> — this executes the <code>screencap</code> command <em>on the phone itself</em>, saving a PNG screenshot to the phone''s storage.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 9:</strong> Runs <code>adb pull</code> to copy that screenshot file from the phone''s storage down to the computer running the script.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 12:</strong> Sends an <code>input tap x y</code> command, simulating a finger tap at pixel coordinates (500, 1000) on the phone''s screen.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 15:</strong> Sends an <code>input swipe x1 y1 x2 y2 duration</code> command, dragging from (300,1500) to (300,500) over 300 milliseconds — a scroll-up gesture.</li>
                        <li><strong>Lines 18–19:</strong> Uses the <code>monkey</code> tool with a package name (<code>com.android.settings</code>) and the <code>LAUNCHER</code> intent category to open that app, exactly like tapping its icon.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <div class="note-warn">⚠️ This requires the Android SDK Platform Tools installed on your computer, a phone with USB debugging enabled, and a real device connection — it cannot run inside the browser-based Pyodide runner, so no Run button is shown for this program.</div>
                </div>

                <div class="oop-block">
                    <h3>🎥 12.3 CCTV / Webcam Automation — Motion Detection with <code>OpenCV</code></h3>
                    <p><code>OpenCV</code> (<code>cv2</code>) is the standard computer-vision library. Pointed at a webcam or an IP camera / CCTV stream, it can compare consecutive frames and automatically save a snapshot the moment something moves — the same core idea behind commercial motion-triggered security cameras.</p>

                    <h4 style="color:var(--blue-lt);font-size:0.8rem;margin-top:0.8rem;margin-bottom:0.4rem;">What this program does:</h4>
                    <p>Opens a camera feed, keeps a reference "background" frame, compares every new frame against it, and saves a timestamped snapshot whenever enough pixels have changed to count as motion.</p>

                    <div class="code-container no-run">
                        <div class="code-header">
                            <span style="color:var(--muted);">python</span>
                            <div class="code-actions">
                                <button class="code-btn copy-btn"><i class="fas fa-copy"></i> Copy</button>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-snippet"><pre><span class="kw">import</span> cv2
<span class="kw">import</span> time
<span class="kw">from</span> datetime <span class="kw">import</span> datetime

<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">cctv_motion_detection</span>():
    cap = cv2.VideoCapture(0)

    ret, first_frame = cap.read()
    first_gray = cv2.cvtColor(first_frame, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)
    first_gray = cv2.GaussianBlur(first_gray, (21, 21), 0)

    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"🎥 Monitoring for motion... press Ctrl+C to stop"</span>)

    <span class="kw">try</span>:
        <span class="kw">while</span> <span class="kw">True</span>:
            ret, frame = cap.read()
            <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="kw">not</span> ret:
                <span class="kw">break</span>

            gray = cv2.cvtColor(frame, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)
            gray = cv2.GaussianBlur(gray, (21, 21), 0)

            delta = cv2.absdiff(first_gray, gray)
            thresh = cv2.threshold(delta, 25, 255, cv2.THRESH_BINARY)[1]

            motion_pixels = cv2.countNonZero(thresh)

            <span class="kw">if</span> motion_pixels &gt; 5000:
                timestamp = datetime.now().strftime(<span class="st">"%Y%m%d_%H%M%S"</span>)
                filename = <span class="st">f"motion_{timestamp}.jpg"</span>
                cv2.imwrite(filename, frame)
                <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"🚨 Motion detected! Saved snapshot: {filename}"</span>)

            first_gray = gray
            time.sleep(0.5)

    <span class="kw">except</span> KeyboardInterrupt:
        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"\n🛑 Stopped monitoring"</span>)
    <span class="kw">finally</span>:
        cap.release()

cctv_motion_detection()</pre></div>
                    </div>

                    <h4 style="color:var(--blue-lt);font-size:0.8rem;margin-top:0.8rem;margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Line-by-line explanation:</h4>
                    <ul class="line-list">
                        <li><strong>Lines 1–3:</strong> Import <code>cv2</code> (OpenCV), <code>time</code> for pausing, and <code>datetime</code> to build timestamped filenames.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 6:</strong> <code>cv2.VideoCapture(0)</code> opens camera index 0 — the first connected camera. An IP/CCTV camera would instead use its RTSP URL string here, e.g. <code>cv2.VideoCapture("rtsp://...")</code>.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 8:</strong> Reads one frame from the camera to use as the starting reference ("what the room normally looks like").</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 9:</strong> Converts that frame from color (BGR) to grayscale — motion detection only needs brightness, not color, which is faster to process.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 10:</strong> Blurs the grayscale frame slightly with a Gaussian blur so tiny sensor noise doesn''t get mistaken for motion.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 14:</strong> Begins an infinite loop that keeps reading frames until the user stops the program or the camera disconnects.</li>
                        <li><strong>Lines 15–17:</strong> Reads the next frame; if <code>ret</code> is <code>False</code> the camera failed to return a frame, so the loop breaks.</li>
                        <li><strong>Lines 19–20:</strong> Applies the exact same grayscale-and-blur treatment to the new frame so it can be fairly compared to the reference.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 22:</strong> <code>cv2.absdiff()</code> computes the pixel-by-pixel absolute difference between the reference frame and the current frame — bright areas mean "this pixel changed a lot".</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 23:</strong> <code>cv2.threshold()</code> converts that difference into pure black-and-white: any pixel that changed by more than 25 becomes white (255), everything else becomes black.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 25:</strong> <code>cv2.countNonZero()</code> counts how many white (changed) pixels exist — a simple measure of "how much motion is happening".</li>
                        <li><strong>Lines 27–31:</strong> If the changed-pixel count crosses the 5000 threshold, build a timestamped filename and save the current color frame as a JPEG snapshot with <code>cv2.imwrite()</code>.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 33:</strong> Updates the reference frame to the current one, so future comparisons are against the most recent "normal" state rather than a stale one.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 34:</strong> Sleeps briefly between checks to limit CPU usage.</li>
                        <li><strong>Lines 36–39:</strong> Catches <code>Ctrl+C</code> gracefully, and the <code>finally</code> block always calls <code>cap.release()</code> to free the camera device.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <div class="note-warn">⚠️ This needs <code>pip install opencv-python</code> and an actual camera or CCTV/RTSP stream — the browser sandbox has no camera access, so this program has no Run button here. Test it locally.</div>
                </div>

                <div class="oop-block">
                    <h3>📥 12.4 Task Automation — Watching a Folder with <code>watchdog</code></h3>
                    <p>A lot of real automation is reactive: "whenever a new file shows up, do something with it." The <code>watchdog</code> library listens to filesystem events in the background and fires your function the instant a file is created, changed, or deleted — no polling loop required.</p>

                    <h4 style="color:var(--blue-lt);font-size:0.8rem;margin-top:0.8rem;margin-bottom:0.4rem;">What this program does:</h4>
                    <p>Watches a folder for new files. The moment a file appears, it automatically moves it into a subfolder named after its extension — a live, always-on version of the file organizer from section 11.2.</p>

                    <div class="code-container no-run">
                        <div class="code-header">
                            <span style="color:var(--muted);">python</span>
                            <div class="code-actions">
                                <button class="code-btn copy-btn"><i class="fas fa-copy"></i> Copy</button>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-snippet"><pre><span class="kw">import</span> time
<span class="kw">from</span> pathlib <span class="kw">import</span> Path
<span class="kw">from</span> watchdog.observers <span class="kw">import</span> Observer
<span class="kw">from</span> watchdog.events <span class="kw">import</span> FileSystemEventHandler

<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="cl">NewFileHandler</span>(FileSystemEventHandler):
    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">on_created</span>(self, event):
        <span class="kw">if</span> event.is_directory:
            <span class="kw">return</span>
        path = Path(event.src_path)
        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"📥 New file detected: {path.name}"</span>)

        target_dir = path.parent / path.suffix.lstrip(<span class="st">"."</span>)
        target_dir.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
        path.rename(target_dir / path.name)
        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"✅ Moved to {target_dir}/"</span>)

<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">folder_watcher_demo</span>():
    watch_path = Path.cwd() / <span class="st">"watched_folder"</span>
    watch_path.mkdir(exist_ok=True)

    event_handler = NewFileHandler()
    observer = Observer()
    observer.schedule(event_handler, str(watch_path), recursive=False)
    observer.start()

    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"👀 Watching {watch_path} for new files (runs 10s)..."</span>)
    time.sleep(10)

    observer.stop()
    observer.join()
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"🧹 Stopped watching"</span>)

folder_watcher_demo()</pre></div>
                    </div>

                    <h4 style="color:var(--blue-lt);font-size:0.8rem;margin-top:0.8rem;margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Line-by-line explanation:</h4>
                    <ul class="line-list">
                        <li><strong>Lines 1–4:</strong> Import <code>time</code> for the demo timer, <code>Path</code> for filesystem paths, and two pieces from <code>watchdog</code>: <code>Observer</code> (runs the background watching thread) and <code>FileSystemEventHandler</code> (the base class you customize to react to events).</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 6:</strong> Defines <code>NewFileHandler</code>, a subclass of <code>FileSystemEventHandler</code>, so we can override just the events we care about.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 7:</strong> <code>on_created()</code> is a method <code>watchdog</code> calls automatically every time a new file or folder appears in the watched directory.</li>
                        <li><strong>Lines 8–9:</strong> If the new item is a directory (not a file), exit early — we only want to react to files.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 10:</strong> Wraps the raw path string from the event into a <code>Path</code> object for easier manipulation.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 11:</strong> Prints which file was just detected.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 13:</strong> Builds a target folder name from the file''s extension (<code>.suffix</code>) with the leading dot stripped, e.g. <code>.txt</code> → <code>txt</code>.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 14:</strong> Creates that target folder if it doesn''t already exist.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 15:</strong> <code>path.rename()</code> moves the file into the target folder — renaming to a new location is how you move files with <code>pathlib</code>.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 18:</strong> Defines the function that sets everything up and runs the watcher.</li>
                        <li><strong>Lines 19–20:</strong> Creates the folder that will be watched, if it doesn''t exist yet.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 22:</strong> Creates an instance of our custom handler.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 23:</strong> Creates an <code>Observer</code> — the object that actually watches the filesystem in a background thread.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 24:</strong> <code>observer.schedule()</code> tells the observer which handler to call, which folder to watch, and whether to watch subfolders too (<code>recursive=False</code> means top level only).</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 25:</strong> Starts the background watching thread.</li>
                        <li><strong>Line 28:</strong> Keeps the main program alive for 10 seconds so the background thread has time to catch events — in production this would run indefinitely.</li>
                        <li><strong>Lines 30–31:</strong> Stops the observer thread and waits for it to fully shut down.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <div class="note-warn">⚠️ Requires <code>pip install watchdog</code>, which is not preloaded in the Pyodide sandbox, and needs a real filesystem to watch — copy this into a local Python environment to run it.</div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="faq-automation" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
            <div class="page-col">
                <div class="section-intro">
                    <h2 id="faq-heading">❓ Frequently Asked Questions — Python Automation</h2>
                    <p>Common questions about automating with Python, answered.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-list">
                    <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)"><h3>What''s the difference between <code>shutil</code> and <code>os</code> for file operations?</h3><span class="faq-toggle">+</span></div><div class="faq-a"><p><code>os</code> provides low‑level system functions. <code>shutil</code> builds on <code>os</code> to provide high‑level operations like copying entire directory trees, moving files across filesystems, and creating archives. Use <code>shutil</code> for common tasks, <code>os</code> for fine‑grained control.</p></div></div>
                    <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)"><h3>How do I run a Python script on a schedule?</h3><span class="faq-toggle">+</span></div><div class="faq-a"><p>Use the <code>schedule</code> library for in‑process scheduling, or use your operating system''s scheduler: <code>cron</code> on Linux/macOS, Task Scheduler on Windows. For production, consider <code>APScheduler</code> or a dedicated orchestration tool like <code>Celery</code>.</p></div></div>
                    <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)"><h3>Is web scraping legal?</h3><span class="faq-toggle">+</span></div><div class="faq-a"><p>Check the website''s <code>robots.txt</code> and terms of service. Scraping public data for personal use is usually fine, but avoid overwhelming servers (use delays) and respect copyright. Always identify your bot with a custom <code>User-Agent</code>.</p></div></div>
                    <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)"><h3>What''s the best library for Excel automation?</h3><span class="faq-toggle">+</span></div><div class="faq-a"><p><code>openpyxl</code> is the most popular for reading/writing <code>.xlsx</code> files. For older <code>.xls</code> files, use <code>xlrd</code> (read) and <code>xlwt</code> (write). For data‑heavy workflows, consider <code>pandas</code> which uses <code>openpyxl</code> or <code>xlrd</code> under the hood.</p></div></div>
                    <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)"><h3>How do I keep my automation scripts secure?</h3><span class="faq-toggle">+</span></div><div class="faq-a"><p>Never hard‑code credentials. Use environment variables, <code>.env</code> files, or a secrets manager. Validate all inputs, especially when using <code>subprocess</code> with <code>shell=True</code>. Run automation scripts with the least privilege necessary.</p></div></div>
                    <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)"><h3>What should I automate first?</h3><span class="faq-toggle">+</span></div><div class="faq-a"><p>Start with tasks you do repeatedly: file renaming, data entry, report generation, or system checks. The best automation is the one that saves you the most time. Start small and iterate.</p></div></div>
                    <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)"><h3>Can I use Python to automate Excel on macOS/Linux?</h3><span class="faq-toggle">+</span></div><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes! <code>openpyxl</code> works on all platforms because it reads/writes the file format directly — no Excel installation needed. For Windows‑specific CO' WHERE title='Automation' AND date='2026-10-30' AND length(body) = 119510;
UPDATE articles SET body = body || 'M automation (controlling the Excel application itself), you''d need <code>win32com</code> which only works on Windows.</p></div></div>
                    <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)"><h3>What''s the difference between Selenium and <code>requests</code> + <code>BeautifulSoup</code>?</h3><span class="faq-toggle">+</span></div><div class="faq-a"><p><code>requests</code> just downloads raw HTML — it never runs JavaScript. Selenium opens a real browser, so it can click buttons, fill forms, wait for JavaScript-rendered content, and log into accounts. Use <code>requests</code> for simple static pages; use Selenium when the page needs interaction or JavaScript to show its data.</p></div></div>
                    <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)"><h3>Do I need a rooted phone to automate it with ADB?</h3><span class="faq-toggle">+</span></div><div class="faq-a"><p>No. Tapping, swiping, screenshotting, and launching apps through <code>adb shell input</code> and <code>monkey</code> all work on a stock, non‑rooted phone — you only need USB debugging enabled in Developer Options. Rooting is only needed for deeper system-level automation.</p></div></div>
                    <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)"><h3>Can Python automation work with real CCTV/IP cameras, not just a webcam?</h3><span class="faq-toggle">+</span></div><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. Most IP cameras and CCTV/NVR systems expose an RTSP stream URL (often something like <code>rtsp://user:pass@camera-ip:554/stream</code>). Pass that URL string straight into <code>cv2.VideoCapture()</code> instead of a camera index, and the rest of the motion-detection code works the same way.</p></div></div>
                    <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)"><h3>Why use <code>watchdog</code> instead of just checking a folder in a loop?</h3><span class="faq-toggle">+</span></div><div class="faq-a"><p>A polling loop (checking every few seconds) wastes CPU and adds delay. <code>watchdog</code> uses your operating system''s native file-event notifications, so your handler fires instantly when a file appears, with near-zero CPU cost while idle.</p></div></div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

