        <section id="borneo" aria-labelledby="borneo-heading">
            <div class="page-col">
                <div class="section-label"><span>🗺️ Bisaya of Borneo</span></div>
                <h2 id="borneo-heading">The Bisaya of Borneo and the Maragtas Tradition</h2>
                <div class="article-body">
                    <p>Across the Sulu Sea and the wider waters of northern Borneo lives another people who also call themselves Bisaya or Bisayah. They are found mainly in the Malaysian states of Sabah and Sarawak and in parts of Brunei. Historically they have been riverine farmers, cultivating rice, sago, and other crops along the lower reaches of major rivers, and many are known for their traditional buffalo-keeping and for their annual rice rituals. Their languages belong to the Austronesian family and show affinities with other northern Bornean groups.</p>

                    <p>The existence of two peoples sharing the same name, separated by hundreds of kilometers of sea, has naturally invited speculation about a historical connection. The most famous expression of that idea is the <strong>Maragtas</strong>, a traditional narrative associated especially with the island of Panay. According to the story, in the thirteenth century ten datus led by Datu Puti fled the oppressive rule of a Bornean ruler named Makatunaw and sailed northward with their followers. Upon reaching Panay they negotiated with the local Ati, led by Marikudo, purchasing the lowlands in exchange for gold and other goods. The newcomers established settlements that became the foundation of later Visayan society in the western Visayas. The story is retold every year in festivals such as the Ati-Atihan of Kalibo, the Dinagyang of Iloilo, and the Binirayan of Antique.</p>

                    <p>Despite its cultural importance, the Maragtas does not stand up well as a literal historical record. The best known written version was compiled by Pedro Alcantara Monteclaro and published in 1907, drawing on earlier oral and written material. Later historians, most famously William Henry Scott, examined its sources and concluded that parts of it rest on unreliable or even fabricated documents. Modern scholarship therefore regards it primarily as a later literary and oral tradition that crystallizes older memories of migration, alliance, and cultural encounter. Linguistic comparison between the Bisaya languages of Borneo and the Visayan languages of the Philippines reveals no especially close relationship beyond the general ties that connect all Austronesian languages of the region. Archaeology likewise shows continuous local development in the Visayas, not a sudden influx of Bornean settlers in the thirteenth century.</p>

                    <p>Nevertheless, the story continues to hold meaning. It expresses a sense of connection to the wider Malay world and provides a dramatic origin narrative that elevates Visayan identity. In this sense the Maragtas functions more as a cultural charter than as a precise chronicle. It also carries a thoughtful moral: the ten datus buy the land rather than seizing it, and the Ati are remembered as the first people of the island. It reminds listeners that the Visayas have long been part of a maritime realm in which people, goods, and stories moved freely across the seas.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="srivijaya" aria-labelledby="srivijaya-heading">
            <div class="page-col">
                <div class="section-label"><span>🌊 Srivijaya Empire</span></div>
                <h2 id="srivijaya-heading">Srivijaya: The Maritime Empire and Its Reach</h2>
                <div class="article-body">
                    <p>To understand possible links between the Visayas and the classical empires of Island Southeast Asia, we must turn to Srivijaya itself. Emerging in the seventh century, with an early inscription dated 683 and its center near present-day Palembang on the island of Sumatra, Srivijaya grew into the dominant maritime power of the western Indonesian archipelago. Its strength rested on control of the Strait of Malacca and other vital sea lanes that carried spices, aromatic woods, gold, tin, textiles, and ceramics between China, India, and the Middle East.</p>

                    <p>Srivijaya was a thalassocracy, an empire of the sea rather than a vast territorial state. Its rulers exercised authority through a combination of naval power, commercial monopolies, diplomatic alliances, and the prestige of Mahayana Buddhism. Chinese pilgrims traveling to India often stopped in Srivijaya to study Buddhist texts, and the empire maintained monasteries and scholarly contacts that reached as far as the Indian subcontinent. At various times its influence extended over parts of the Malay Peninsula, western Java, and coastal Borneo.</p>

                    <p>Direct political control over the Philippine islands is not documented in the surviving records of Srivijaya. The empire''s known tributaries and dependencies do not include the Visayas. Yet the Philippines clearly took part in the same maritime trading networks. Goods and ideas moved along routes that linked the South China Sea with the Java Sea and the Strait of Malacca, and cultural influences from the Indianized kingdoms of Sumatra and the Malay world reached Philippine shores.</p>

                    <p>One of the most important pieces of evidence is the <strong>Laguna Copperplate Inscription</strong>, discovered in Luzon and dated to the year 900. Written in a mixture of Old Malay with Sanskrit and some Old Javanese and Old Tagalog terms, it records the clearing of a debt and mentions a place called Sribuza, widely understood as a reference to Srivijaya. The document shows that by the early tenth century at least some Philippine polities were familiar with the political and commercial world centered on Sumatra and used related legal and administrative concepts.</p>

                    <p>Archaeological finds in the Visayas and neighboring areas further illustrate these connections. Chinese ceramics of Tang and Song date appear in coastal sites, as do gold ornaments whose styles show affinities with objects found across Maritime Southeast Asia. The golden Tara image from Agusan, in northern Mindanao, reflects Hindu-Buddhist artistic influence. Certain forms of jewelry, including the distinctive lingling-o earrings, occur in both Philippine and Indonesian contexts. Indianized titles such as raja appear alongside the indigenous term datu, suggesting that local leaders sometimes adopted foreign symbols of authority to enhance their prestige.</p>

                    <p>These contacts were real and significant, yet they do not support the stronger claim that the Visayan people themselves originated as colonists from Srivijaya, or that the islands formed a formal province of the empire. The evidence points instead to participation in a shared maritime world in which influence flowed through trade, intermarriage among elites, and the selective adoption of prestigious foreign practices. When Srivijaya declined under pressure from the Chola dynasty of South India in the eleventh century and later from rising Javanese powers, the commercial networks it had dominated did not disappear; they reorganized under new centers, and Philippine communities kept trading and absorbing new influences from the evolving Malay and Javanese worlds.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <div class="ad-slot page-col">
            <ins class="adsbygoogle"
                 style="display:block"
                 data-ad-client="ca-pub-6428799122405621"
                 data-ad-slot="8844498660"
                 data-ad-format="auto"
                 data-full-width-responsive="true"></ins>
